See [Cut false starts](/cleanup/false-starts/).

## It looks at rendered frames

The Assistant checks its work by rendering a frame of the finished timeline, drawn the way an export draws it. It's told to look before and after a visual edit. It renders at the playhead unless it picks a time, and it's told to keep the image small.

Rendering writes a picture file without asking. By default that's a temporary file.

:::note
The frames it renders go to Anthropic, along with your messages and the project's details. See [Assistant privacy](/assistant/privacy/).
:::
### Projects and navigation

It can open, create and save projects, move the playhead, set the in and out range, change the selection, and enter or leave a nest. It can also make a [new project from a range](/projects/new-project-from-range/) or a [vertical version](/framing/make-vertical-version/).

Saving asks first. Making a new project from a range or a vertical version asks first when it names where to save. Moving the playhead, setting the range and changing the selection aren't edits, so they make no undo step.

A project it opens or creates gets a window of its own, and the Assistant stays with the project it started in.
Compaction loses the frames the Assistant rendered earlier, so it renders again when it needs to look. Each clearing also adds to your bill. See [Long conversations](/assistant/context-and-compaction/).

Under the buttons is what the Assistant has cost this month, with a note that the figure is Frame Job's own estimate. See [What it costs](/assistant/cost/).

The ⓘ sums up what's sent to Anthropic, that it's billed to your key, that the Assistant asks before writing a file, where the key is kept, the blank **Model:** default and the two chat settings.

## Transcription

This section holds your AssemblyAI key and region. **File ▸ Generate Captions…** uses them with AssemblyAI chosen, and so does **Clip ▸ Remove Filler Words…**. Captions made on your Mac need no key. See [Your AssemblyAI key](/captions/assemblyai-key/).
